             (:domain "decathlon tenth event" :code "(define alphabet (list 'a 'b 'c 'd 'e 'f 'g 'h 'i 'j 'k))\n(display (list 'letter-j (tenth alphabet)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "π_10 projection" :code "(define (pi10 t) (list-ref t 9))"))
     :explanation "In list, tenth names position 10. In athletics, the tenth event completes a decathlon. π_10 projects the tenth coordinate. All three share: named handle for position 10."
     :meta "The shape transfers where position 10 has meaning; beyond 10 the language expects list-ref."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(display (tenth (iota 12)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (tenth (iota 20)) (list-ref (iota 20) 9)))\n(newline)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "'honest-null-math ; the index-equivalence claim (tenth = π_10 = list-ref … 9) is a CS naming-alias property, not algebra." :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define batch (list (iota 10) (iota 12) (iota 25)))\n(display (map tenth batch))\n(newline)" :tags (cs program)))
     :claim "tenth returns exactly the tenth element of a list of length ≥ 10."
     :programs (
       (:code "(display (tenth (iota 15)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (tenth (iota 15)) (list-ref (iota 15) 9)))\n(newline)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ list xs with |xs| ≥ 10. tenth(xs) = xs[9]"
     :meta "The pattern is index-equivalent named access. CS pattern name: named accessor. What would falsify: off-by-one. See book-of-code ch07."
     :eng "Stable named form of index 9; last of the SRFI-1 accessor series."
     :math "tenth = π_10 on 10+ tuples."
     :cs "Named accessor; language stops the named series at ten.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define scores (iota 10 100))\n(define last-score (tenth scores))\n(define first-score (first scores))\n(display (list 'delta (- last-score first-score)))\n(newline)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "'honest-null-math ; delta-of-endpoints uses subtraction as the algebra (that lives in arithmetic); tenth's role is just projection." :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (dashboard series)\n  (list 'opening (first series) 'quartile (third series) 'midpoint (fifth series) 'closing (tenth series)))\n(display (dashboard (iota 10 200)))\n(newline)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :base "tenth returns the tenth element (last of the SRFI-1 named accessors)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define scores (iota 10 100))\n(define last-score (tenth scores))\n(define first-score (first scores))\n(display (list 'delta (- last-score first-score)))\n(newline)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "endpoints delta — pair first and tenth to compute a range delta"
     :meta "The composition pairs first with tenth to derive a delta. What emerges is the delta-of-endpoints family: change between two named boundary positions. See book-of-composition ch08.")
)
)

(verb :name "list-tail" :std "R7RS-small"  :library "list" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(list-tail lst k) -> list"
  :summary "Drop the first k elements. R7RS §6.4."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(list-tail (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)" :note "(3 4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define offsets (iota 10))\n(list-tail offsets 5)" :note "Skip the first half: (5 6 7 8 9).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (pages xs per)\n  (if (null? xs) '()\n    (cons (if (< (length xs) per) xs (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per))))\n          (pages (if (< (length xs) per) '() (list-tail xs per)) per))))\n(pages (iota 7) 3)" :note "Paginate into 3-per-page: ((0 1 2) (3 4 5) (6))."))
  :provenance "R7RS §6.4; added 2026-07-18 finalization"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (nth-cdr-equivalent xs k)\n  (equal? (list-tail xs k)\n          (let loop ((n k) (r xs)) (if (or (= n 0) (null? r)) r (loop (- n 1) (cdr r))))))\n(display (map (lambda (k) (nth-cdr-equivalent '(a b c d e) k)) '(0 1 2 3 5)))\n(newline)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (safe-tail xs k)\n  (cond ((< k 0) 'negative-drop-illegal)\n        ((> k (length xs)) '())\n        (else (list-tail xs k))))\n(display (list (safe-tail '(a b c) 5) (safe-tail '(a b c) -1) (safe-tail '(a b c) 2)))\n(newline)" :tags (cs program)))
     :problem "The operator is processing a CSV file where the first row is a header and only the data rows should be handed downstream. Candidates: list-tail lst 1 (drop the header), (cdr lst) (drops one, but the k form generalizes), (filter (lambda (x) …) lst) (wrong shape). For 'drop the first k,' list-tail is honest."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "eng: drop CSV header row. math: list-tail = k-fold cdr — this is the definitional identity. cs: bounds-safe wrapper that translates out-of-range k into semantic sentinels."
     :meta "Why list-tail and not cdr: same for k=1; list-tail generalizes to any k without nesting cdrs.")
    (:eng "Prefer list-tail when the drop count is variable; prefer cdr when k=1 is idiomatic."
     :math "list-tail(xs, k) = drop k elements from the front."
     :cs "Related patterns: [k:] in Python, drop in Haskell, skip in LINQ.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A prefix drop: skip the first k elements of a sequence and return the remaining suffix."
     :rungs ((:domain "list" :code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "skip the intro of a book" :code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "shift by k in a stream" :code "(define (drop k xs) (if (or (null? xs) (= k 0)) xs (drop (- k 1) (cdr xs))))"))
     :explanation "In list, list-tail skips k elements. Skipping the preface of a book gives the reader the main content. In streams, shifting by k discards k elements. All three share: forget the first k, keep the rest."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a prefix is discarded to focus on the remainder."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define xs (iota 5))\n(display (list-tail xs 0))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 2))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 5))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'len (length (list-tail xs 2)) 'expected (- (length xs) 2)))\n(newline)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (drop-additive? xs a b)\n  (equal? (list-tail (list-tail xs a) b)\n          (list-tail xs (+ a b))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (drop-additive? '(a b c d e f g) (car p) (cadr p)))\n              '((0 3) (2 2) (3 1))))\n(newline)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (shares-tail? xs k)\n  (let ((t (list-tail xs k)))\n    (eq? t (list-tail xs k))))\n(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(display (shares-tail? xs 2))\n(newline)" :tags (cs program)))
     :claim "list-tail returns a list of length max(0, |xs|-k) whose contents are xs[k..]; list-tail with k=0 returns xs unchanged."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define xs (iota 5))\n(display (list-tail xs 0))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 2))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 5))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'len (length (list-tail xs 2)) 'expected (- (length xs) 2)))\n(newline)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ xs k. 0 ≤ k ≤ |xs| ⇒ |list-tail(xs, k)| = |xs| - k; list-tail(xs, 0) = xs"
     :meta "eng: length formula. math: additivity — dropping a then b is dropping a+b. cs: structural sharing — the returned tail is eq? across calls (no copy)."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that list-tail is O(k) and does not copy — it returns a suffix that shares structure with xs."
     :math "list-tail is the composition of cdr k times."
     :cs "Because Scheme lists are singly-linked, list-tail is O(k) time and O(1) space.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define pages (list 'cover 'preface 'ch1 'ch2 'ch3 'ch4))\n(define chapters (list-tail pages 2))\n(define (paginate xs per-page) (if (null? xs) (list) (cons (if (< (length xs) per-page) xs (list-tail (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per-page))) 0)) (paginate (if (< (length xs) per-page) (list) (list-tail xs per-page)) per-page))))\n(display chapters)\n(newline)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (sliding-suffixes xs)\n  (let loop ((k 0) (acc '()))\n    (if (> k (length xs)) (reverse acc) (loop (+ k 1) (cons (list-tail xs k) acc)))))\n(display (sliding-suffixes '(a b c d)))\n(newline)" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (window xs w step)\n  (let loop ((i 0) (acc '()))\n    (if (> (+ i w) (length xs))\n      (reverse acc)\n      (loop (+ i step)\n            (cons (reverse (list-tail (reverse (list-tail xs i)) (- (length xs) i w))) acc)))))\n(display (window (iota 8) 3 2))\n(newline)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :base "list-tail drops the first k elements."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define pages (list 'cover 'preface 'ch1 'ch2 'ch3 'ch4))\n(define chapters (list-tail pages 2))\n(define (paginate xs per-page) (if (null? xs) (list) (cons (if (< (length xs) per-page) xs (list-tail (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per-page))) 0)) (paginate (if (< (length xs) per-page) (list) (list-tail xs per-page)) per-page))))\n(display chapters)\n(newline)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "eng: header-skip and pagination. math: complete family of tail-suffixes (every drop from 0 to |xs|). cs: fixed-size sliding window with step — same shape as time-series windowing."
     :meta "The composition uses list-tail as the prefix-skip step. What emerges is the header/body separation family: split off metadata to isolate content. Same shape as HTTP header/body splitting, as CSV header skipping. See book-of-composition ch10.")
)
)

(verb :name "list-copy" :std "R7RS-small"  :library "list" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(list-copy lst) -> list"
  :summary "A shallow copy of lst. R7RS §6.4."
  :provenance "R7RS §6.4; added 2026-07-18 finalization"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(list-copy (list 1 2 3))" :note "(1 2 3) — a fresh copy.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(eq? original twin)" :note "#f — same content, different list.")
  )
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin) 'len-preserved (= (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define original '(a b c d))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'content-eq (equal? original twin) 'identity-distinct (not (eq? original twin)) 'spine-fresh (not (eq? (cdr original) (cdr twin)))))\n(newline)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define original (list (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2)))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'top-fresh (not (eq? original twin)) 'element-shared (eq? (car original) (car twin)) 'shallow-copy-witness 'true))\n(newline)" :tags (cs program)))
     :problem "The operator has a list to hand off to a callee that may mutate it (via a hypothetical mutation verb), and the operator wants the original preserved. Candidates: list-copy (fresh top-level structure), passing the same reference (unsafe if callee mutates), deep-copy (overkill unless the ELEMENTS are also mutable). For a shallow protective copy, list-copy is the honest verb."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin)))\n(newline)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Line 1 defines the original. Line 2 makes a shallow copy. Lines 3-4 confirm they are equal? but not eq?. The audit tape shows two independent list handles with matching content."
     :meta "Why list-copy and not passing the reference: the reference is aliased; any downstream mutation is observed by all holders.")
    (:eng "Use list-copy at handoff boundaries when the callee is untrusted or the caller wants to preserve the original."
     :math "The result is the same sequence as a value; equality holds under equal?, not under eq?."
     :cs "Related patterns: list(original) in Python, ArrayList copy constructor in Java. Shallow copy shares element identity but not spine.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A defensive copy: produce an independent handle to the same content so that mutations to one do not affect the other."
     :rungs ((:domain "list" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "photocopy the ledger" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "identity on values, distinctness on identities" :code "(define (twin xs) (map (lambda (x) x) xs))"))
     :explanation "In list, list-copy makes a fresh spine. Photocopying a ledger gives you the same values in a physically distinct document. In math, the identity function on a value copies the value into a new container. All three share: same content, different container."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a value has both a content aspect and an identity aspect and the client needs the content without the identity."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal (equal? original twin) 'not-eq (not (eq? original twin)) 'len-eq (= (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (copy-invariants xs)\n  (let ((c (list-copy xs)))\n    (and (equal? xs c)\n         (= (length xs) (length c))\n         (or (null? xs) (not (eq? xs c))))))\n(display (and (copy-invariants '(1 2 3)) (copy-invariants '()) (copy-invariants '(a b c d e f g))))\n(newline)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define original (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define c1 (list-copy original))\n(define c2 (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'both-content-eq (and (equal? original c1) (equal? original c2)) 'c1-and-c2-distinct (not (eq? c1 c2))))\n(newline)" :tags (cs program)))
     :claim "list-copy returns a list equal to the original but with a distinct spine: equal? is #t, eq? is #f; the length is preserved."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin) 'len (list (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ list xs. list-copy(xs) equal? xs ∧ list-copy(xs) not eq? xs ∧ |list-copy(xs)| = |xs|"
     :meta "The pattern is content-preserving, identity-distinct copy. CS pattern name: shallow copy. What would falsify: eq? equality, or an inequal-equal? result. See book-of-code ch07 on equality and identity."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is safe handoff without downstream mutation risk."
     :math "Distinct identity, equal value."
     :cs "Shallow copy: spine is copied, elements are aliased.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (with-list-arg xs body-fn)\n  (let ((local (list-copy xs)))\n    (body-fn local)))\n(define original (list 'a 'b 'c))\n(display (with-list-arg original (lambda (l) (list 'inside l))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'original-still original))\n(newline)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (all-permutation-inputs base)\n  (map (lambda (_) (list-copy base)) '(1 2 3)))\n(define bases (all-permutation-inputs '(a b c)))\n(display (list 'all-equal (every (lambda (b) (equal? b '(a b c))) bases) 'all-distinct-refs (not (eq? (car bases) (cadr bases)))))\n(newline)" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (safe-map-batch xs procs)\n  (map (lambda (proc) (proc (list-copy xs))) procs))\n(define batch (safe-map-batch '(1 2 3)\n                              (list (lambda (l) (cons 0 l))\n                                    (lambda (l) (reverse l))\n                                    (lambda (l) (length l)))))\n(display batch)\n(newline)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :base "list-copy makes a fresh spine over the same elements."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (with-list-arg xs body-fn)\n  (let ((local (list-copy xs)))\n    (body-fn local)))\n(define original (list 'a 'b 'c))\n(display (with-list-arg original (lambda (l) (list 'inside l))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'original-still original))\n(newline)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "isolation wrapper — a helper that gives the body function its own independent list handle so the original is unaffected by any (hypothetical) local mutation"
     :meta "The composition wraps list-copy around a body call. What emerges is the defensive-copy family: functional-boundary safety. Same shape as immutable-wrapper patterns, as passing-by-value contracts. See book-of-composition ch11.")
)
)

